          %h2#idea Idea
          %h3#IdeaGeneral General
          %p
            %em Quantum computation
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computation"} computation
            in terms of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theory
            = succeed "," do
              %em quantum computers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+systems"} physical systems
            for which phenomena of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            are not negligible:
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+systems"} quantum systems
            such as consisting of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+particles"} fundamental particles
            like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/photons"} photons
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electrons"} electrons
            but also often of quantum-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasiparticles"} quasiparticles
            such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cooper+pairs"} Cooper pairs
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superconductors"} superconductors
          %p
            In terms of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computational+trilogy"} computational trilogy
            = succeed "/(" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+logic"} quantum logic
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+linear+type+theory"} dependent
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+type+theory"} linear type theory
          %p
            To the extent that quantum computation involves
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
            (generally to read out results, but often also as a kind of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+logic+gate"} quantum logic gate
            in its own right) it is a form of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nondeterministic+computation"} nondeterministic computation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+theory"} stochastic
            nature of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state+collapse"} quantum state collapse
            during
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
          %p
            However, away from
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gates"} quantum gates
            are understood to implement
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+operators"} unitary operators
            on a given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} of quantum states
            (fundamentally due to the unitary nature of time-evolution as given by the
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+equation"} Schrödinger equation
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reversible+computation"} reversible computation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+error+correction"} quantum error correction
            and/or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+computation"} topological quantum stabilization
          %p
            Specifically,
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+computation"} topological quantum computation
            is (or is meant to be) quantum computation implemented on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+systems"} physical systems
            governed by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+field+theory"} topological quantum field theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anyon"} anyon
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/defects"} defects
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+order"} topologically ordered
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+gap"} gapped
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+states"} ground states
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+materials"} quantum materials
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+phases+of+matter"} topological phases of matter
          %h3#ClassicalControlQuantumData Classical control, quantum data
          %p
            Any practical quantum computer will be
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classically+controlled+quantum+computation"} classically controlled
            (an idea that may be traced back to
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Knill96"} Knill 96
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Omer03"} Ömer 03
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Devitt14"} Devitt 14
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/ClassicallyControlledQuantumComputation.jpg", :width => "400px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                (from
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#Miszczak11"} Miszczak 11, Fig. 9
          %p
            Already for presently available
            %a{:href => "#ReferencesNISQ"} NISQ
            machines, classically-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parameterized+quantum+systems"} parameterized quantum systems
            have been considered in contexts such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+machine+learning"} quantum machine learning
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#BenedettiLloydSackFiorentini19"}> BLSF 19
            \&amp;rbrack;:
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/HybridQuantumComputing.jpg", :width => "350px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                (from
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#BenedettiLloydSackFiorentini19"} BLSF 19, Fig. 1
          %p#DynamicLifting
            Here the classical control itself is generally conditioned on
            = succeed "-outcomes" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+error+correction"} quantum error correction
            — available only during runtime, a mechanism that in the
            = succeed "-community" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+programming+languages"} quantum programming languages
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quipper"} Quipper
            = succeed "\u201D:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dynamic+lifting"} dynamic lifting
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px -10px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/SQRAMschema-WithDynLifting.jpg", :width => "500px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                (SQRAM model adapted from
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#NagarajanPapanikolaouWilliams07"} Nagarajan, Papanikolaou and Williams 2007, Fig 1
          %p#NeedForClassicalControl
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Devitt14"}> Devitt 14
            ,
            = succeed ":&rbrack;" do
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1405.4943#page=2"} p. 2
          %blockquote
            %p
              Irrespective of the actual quantum code chosen to protect a quantum computer, it is well known that operating such as system requires extensive classical control infrastructure. This is not simply related to the control of the physical device hardware needed to operate a qubit (lasers, signal generators etc…), but it is also required to decode
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+error+correction"} error correction
              information produced by the computer.
          %p
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#KESWDSC23"}> KESWDSC 23
            ,
            = succeed ":&rbrack;" do
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2311.07121#page=2"} p. 2
          %blockquote
            %p
              Apart from the requirements on the quantum hardware, the successful execution of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+error+correction"} QEC codes
              depends on the classical control system. The classical control system is responsible for all classical aspects of quantum computing and includes executing the quantum control and measurement signals, acquiring the readout signals, and performing classical processing operations, all in a synchronized manner. In QEC, the control system is also responsible for mapping the local physical measurements into logical measurement results or logical Pauli frame updates through an algorithmic procedure called decoding. To reach useful quantum computation with QEC, i.e., perform fault tolerant non-Clifford gates, the control unit is required to perform quantum gates that depend on the decoding output. Moreover, to prevent a diverging classical calculation latency, it is crucial for the control system to close a tight loop, with ultra-low latency, from the physical quantum measurement through the classical decoding procedure to a conditional feed-forward quantum operation. Thus, the success of fault-tolerant quantum computation depends on minimizing the QEC feed-forward latency.
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/CRKAW-QECCP.jpg", :width => "700px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                (QECCP schematics fom
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#KESWDSC23"} KESWDSC 23, Fig. 2
          %p
            For more on technical details of classical control requirements see also
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#CRKAW24"} CRKAW 24
          %p
            Generally, see the list of references
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#ReferencesClassicallyControlledQuantumComputing"} below
          %h3#quantum_languages_and_quantum_circuits Quantum languages and quantum circuits
          %p
            A natural way to understand (via
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computational+trilogism"} computational trilogism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+programming+languages"} quantum programming languages
            is as
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+type+theory"} linear type theory
            (
            %a{:href => "#Pratt92"}> Pratt 92
            , for more see at
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+logic"} quantum logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            in non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+categories"} symmetric monoidal categories
            (
            %a{:href => "#AbramskyCoecke04"}> Abramsky &amp; Coecke 04
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#AbramskyDuncan05"} Abramsky &amp; Duncan 05
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Duncan06"} Duncan 06
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#LagoFaffian12"} Lago-Faffian 12
          %p
            The corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagrams"} string diagrams
            are known as
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+circuit+diagrams"} quantum circuit diagrams
          %p
            In fact, languages for
            %a{:href => "#ClassicalControlQuantumData"} classically controlled quantum computation
            should be based on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+linear+type+theory"} dependent linear type theory
            (
            %a{:href => "dependent+linear+type+theory#Vakar14"}> Vakar 14
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "dependent+linear+type+theory#Vakar15"} Vakar 15
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "dependent+linear+type+theory#Vakar17"} Vakar 17, Sec. 3
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "dependent+linear+type+theory#Lundfall17"} Lundfall 17
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "dependent+linear+type+theory#Lundfall18"} Lundfall 18
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "dependent+linear+type+theory#Schreiber14"} Schreiber 14
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indexed+monoidal+categories"} indexed monoidal categories
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th classical control
                %th quantum data
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+type+theory"} intuitionistic types
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+linear+type+theory"} dependent linear types
          %p
            This idea of classically controlled quantum programming via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+linear+type+theory"} dependent linear type theory
            has been implemented in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QML"} QML
