          %h2#references References
          %h3#general_2 General
          %p The idea of quantum computation was first expressed in:
          %ul
            %li#Manin1980
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yuri+I.+Manin"}> Yuri I. Manin
                , Introduction to:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Computable and Uncomputable
                = succeed "&rbrack;," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Manin-1980.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Mathematics as Metaphor: Selected essays of Yuri I. Manin
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 20
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/cworks-20/"} ISBN:978-0-8218-4331-4
              %blockquote
                %p Perhaps, for a better understanding of &amp;lbrack;molecular biology&amp;rbrack;, we need a mathematical theory of quantum automata.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Benioff"}> Paul Benioff
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The computer as a physical system: A microscopic quantum mechanical Hamiltonian model of computers as represented by Turing machines
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01011339"} doi:10.1007/BF01011339
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Benioff"}> Paul Benioff
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantum Mechanical Models of Turing Machines That Dissipate No Energy
                %strong 48
                1581 (1982) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.48.1581"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRevLett.48.1581
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#Feynman82
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Feynman"}> Richard Feynman
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Simulating physics with computers
                %strong 21
                (1982) 467–488 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02650179"}> doi:10.1007/BF02650179
                \&amp;rbrack;
              %blockquote
                %p “because nature isn’t classical, dammit, if you want to make a simulation of nature, you’d better make it quantum mechanical”
          %p
            First ideas of a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+Turing+machine"} quantum Turing machine
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                David Z. Albert,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On quantum-mechanical automata
                %strong 98
                5–6 (1983) 249-252 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0375-9601(83)90863-0"}> doi:10.1016/0375-9601(83)90863-0
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Deutsch"}> David Deutsch
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantum theory, the Church–Turing principle and the universal quantum computer
                %strong 400
                1818 (1985) 97-117 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1098/rspa.1985.0070"}> doi:10.1098/rspa.1985.0070
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.daviddeutsch.org.uk/wp-content/deutsch85.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The notion of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/controlled+quantum+gate"}> controlled
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+logic+gates"} quantum logic gates
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+circuits"} quantum circuits
            was first made explicit in:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Feynman"}> Richard Feynman
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantum mechanical computers
                %strong 16
                (1986) 507–531 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01886518"}> doi:10.1007/BF01886518
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+E.+Deutsch"}> David E. Deutsch
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantum computational networks
                %strong 425
                1868 (1989) 73-90 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1098/rspa.1989.0099"}> doi:10.1098/rspa.1989.0099
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.jstor.org/stable/2398494"} jstor:2398494
          %p
            The terminology
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/q-bit"} q-bit
            goes back to:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Benjamin+Schumacher"}> Benjamin Schumacher
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quantum coding
              %strong 51
              (1995) 2738 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevA.51.2738"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRevA.51.2738
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p The potential practical relevance of quantum computation was highlighted early on via toy examples
          %p
            such as the
            = succeed ":" do
