          %h3#ReferencesNISQ Noisy intermediate-scale quantum (NISQ) computing
          %p The origin of the notion:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Nikolaj Moll et al.,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantum optimization using variational algorithms on near-term quantum devices
                %strong 3
                3 (2018) &amp;lbrack
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.01022"}> arXiv:1710.01022
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1088/2058-9565/aab822"} doi:10.1088/2058-9565/aab822
            %li#Preskill18
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Preskill"}> John Preskill
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantum Computing in the NISQ era and beyond
                %strong 2
                79 (2018) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1801.00862"}> arXiv:1801.00862
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.22331/q-2018-08-06-79"} doi:10.22331/q-2018-08-06-79
          %p#NISQLimitations
            On the limitations of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+error+correction"}> quantum error-corrected
            ) NISQ machines in achieving any
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+advantage"} quantum advantage
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Subhash Kak:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Prospects for Quantum Computing
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0902.4884"} arXiv:0902.4884
            %li
              %p
                Mikhail Dyakonov:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Prospects for quantum computing: extremely doubtful
                %strong 33
                (2014) 1460357 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.3629"}> arXiv:1401.3629
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/S2010194514603573"} doi:10.1142/S2010194514603573
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gil+Kalai"}> Gil Kalai
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Argument against Quantum Computers
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantum, Probability, Logic – The Work and Influence of Itamar Pitowsky
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.02499"} arXiv:1908.02499
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-34316-3_18"} doi:10.1007/978-3-030-34316-3_18
              %blockquote
                %p
                  A common interpretation of the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/threshold+theorem"} threshold theorem
                  is that it shows that large-scale quantum computers are possible in principle. A more careful interpretation is that if we can control noisy intermediate-scale quantum systems well enough, then we can build large-scale universal quantum computers. As we will see, there are good reasons for why we cannot control the quality of noisy intermediate-scale quantum systems well enough.
            %li
              %p
                Daniel Koch, Brett Martin, Saahil Patel, Laura Wessing, and Paul M. Alsing,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Demonstrating NISQ era challenges in algorithm design on IBM’s 20 qubit quantum computer
                %strong 10
                095101 (2020) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1063/5.0015526"}> doi:10.1063/5.0015526
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#LeymannBarzen20
              %p
                Frank Leymann, Johanna Barzen,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The bitter truth about gate-based quantum algorithms in the NISQ era
                %strong 5
                044007 (2020) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/2058-9565/abae7d"}> doi:10.1088/2058-9565/abae7d
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                Mikhail Dyakonov:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Will We Ever Have a Quantum Computer
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-42019-2"} doi:10.1007/978-3-030-42019-2
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-030-42019-2.pdf"} pdf
              %blockquote
                %p (the author’s conclusion is: “No.”)
            %li#Ezratty
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Olivier+Ezratty"}> Olivier Ezratty
                ,
                %em Where are we heading with NISQ?
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.09518"}> arXiv:2305.09518
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://inspirehep.net/literature/2660378"} InSpire:2660378
              %blockquote
                %p “no one has yet successfully implemented a use case matching the original definition of the NISQ regime.”
                %p
                  “Totally outside the NISQ relevant algorithms class are integer and discrete log factoring algorithms &amp;lbrack;
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shor%27s+algorithm"}> Shor's algorithm
                  \&amp;rbrack;, oracle based search algorithms &amp;lbrack;
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grover%27s+algorithm"}> Grover's algorithm
                  \&amp;rbrack;, and all algorithms relying on a quantum Fourier transform”
                %p “most NISQ experiments have been run with fewer than 30 qubits and should therefore better be labelled as “pre-NISQ”. While they are elegant proofs of concepts, they do not yet demonstrate any speed up over classical computing”
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Olivier+Ezratty"}> Olivier Ezratty
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Where are we heading with NISQ?
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.oezratty.net/wordpress/2023/where-are-we-heading-with-nisq/"} web
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.oezratty.net/wordpress/2023/where-are-we-heading-with-nisq/?output=pdf"} pdf
              %blockquote
                %p NISQ is not at all ready for prime time quantum computing despite all the fuss about “quantum computing being business ready”. Not only have we not yet reached any quantum computing advantage, but in many cases, even if it worked, the most common NISQ algorithms using a variational approach, have prohibitively long execution times particularly in the promising chemical simulations domain.
            %li
              %p
                Jonathan Wei Zhong Lau, Kian Hwee Lim, Harshank Shrotriya, Leong Chuan Kwek,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em NISQ computing: where are we and where do we go?
                %strong 32
                27 (2022) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s43673-022-00058-z"}> doi:10.1007/s43673-022-00058-z
                \&amp;rbrack;
              %blockquote
                %p
                  However, quantum computers are still far from achieving all that they have promised. While early-stage quantum computers have been developed, the problems of noisy calculations and scalability of quantum computers still plague the field. As opposed to the far future, where quantum computers can be as big as we wish them to be and are capable of performing fully controllable operations (termed as the fault-tolerant era), we are currently working in the
                  %em Noisy Intermediate-Scale Quantum
                  (NISQ) era, which is an operational definition that implies that the quantum computers available to us now are subject to substantial error rates and they are constrained in size (in terms of the number of qubits). While it is already a scientific achievement to get to this stage, such quantum computers are still incapable of showing any significant advantages over classical computers. As such, some people in the community have already expressed fear that there will be a ‘Quantum winter’, or a scenario where quantum computing devices remain noisy and are unable to scale up in terms of qubit size, preventing us from ever achieving a meaningful advantage over classical computers.
            %li#Preskill23
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Preskill"}> John Preskill
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Crossing the Quantum Chasm: From NISQ to Fault Tolerance
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://theory.caltech.edu/~preskill/talks/Preskill-Q2B-2023"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Preskill-Crossing.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
              %blockquote
                %p “there is no proposed application of NISQ computing with commercial value for which quantum advantage has been demonstrated”
                %p “Nor are there persuasive theoretical arguments indicating that commercially viable applications will be found that do not use quantum error-correcting codes and fault-tolerant quantum computing.”
            %li#HHT23
              %p
                Torsten Hoefler, Thomas Haener,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Matthias+Troyer"} Matthias Troyer
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Disentangling Hype from Practicality: On Realistically Achieving Quantum Advantage
                %strong 66
                5 (May 2023) 82-87 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.00523"}> arXiv:2307.00523
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://dl.acm.org/doi/pdf/10.1145/3571725"} acm pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1145/3571725"} doi:10.1145/3571725
              %blockquote
                %p “A large range of problem areas with quadratic quantum speedups, such as many current machine learning training approaches, accelerating drug design and protein folding with Grover’s algorithm, speeding up Monte Carlo simulations through quantum walks, as well as more traditional scientific computing simulations including the solution of many non-linear systems of equations, such as fluid dynamics in the turbulent regime, weather, and climate simulations will not achieve quantum advantage with current quantum algorithms in the foreseeable future.”
            %li
              %p
                Edd Gent:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantum Computing’s Hard, Cold Reality Check
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://spectrum.ieee.org/quantum-computing-skeptics"} webpage
            %li#Waintal24
              %p
                Xavier Waintal:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Quantum House Of Cards
                %strong 121
                1 (2024) e2313269120 [
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2313269120"}> doi:10.1073/pnas.2313269120
                ,
                = succeed "]" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.17570"} arXiv:2312.17570
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jens+Eisert"}> Jens Eisert
                ,
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Preskill"} John Preskill
                %em Mind the gaps: The fraught road to quantum advantage
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.19928"}> arXiv:2510.19928
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p On the resulting drop in investment:
          %ul
            %li#McKenzie2024
              James McKenzie:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quantum winter? No chance!
              %strong 37
              5 (2024) 41 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/2058-7058/37/05/32"}> doi:10.1088/2058-7058/37/05/32
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p More optimistic outlook:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                S. G. Gill et al.:
                %em Quantum Computing: Vision and Challenges
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.02240"}> arXiv:2403.02240
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Preskill"}> John Preskill
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Beyond NISQ: The Megaquop Machine
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://q2b.qcware.com/2024-conferences/silicon-valley/"} Q2B 2024 Silicon Valley
                (Dec. 2024) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://www.preskill.caltech.edu/talks/Preskill-Q2B-2024.pdf"}> pdf
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://quantumfrontiers.com/2024/12/14/beyond-nisq-the-megaquop-machine/"} webpage
          %p More concerned outlook:
          %div{:style => "float:right;margin:-15px 0px 00px 20px;"}
            %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/E98zLxB7EQs?t=85"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/DARPA-QBI-IFItTurnsOutNotPossible.jpg", :width => "230"}/
          %ul
            %li#DarpaQBI
              %p
                DARPA:
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://www.darpa.mil/work-with-us/quantum-benchmarking-initiative"} Quantum Benchmarjing Initiative
              %blockquote
                %p “to determine if it’s possible to build an industrially useful quantum computer much faster than conventional predictions. Specifically, QBI is designed to rigorously verify and validate if any quantum computing approach can achieve utility-scale operation — meaning its computational value exceeds its cost — by the year 2033.”
                %p
                  The
                  %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/E98zLxB7EQs"} accompanying video
                  says at
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/E98zLxB7EQs?t=85"} 1:25
                %p “If it turns out, as a lot of us suspect, that that’s really not possible, and it really won’t do something useful, or there isn’t a path, we need to know that, too.”
